Hi,

Power11 PHYP adds Virtual Ethernet multi-queue (MQ) RX: multiple
logical-LAN RX queues, per-queue buffer posting, and completion
delivery. Guest Linux did not use that; ibmveth still registered one
RX queue even when PHYP was MQ-capable.

This series adds the ibmveth MQ client for net-next. When PHYP
advertises IBMVETH_ILLAN_RX_MULTI_QUEUE_SUPPORT via H_ILLAN_ATTRIBUTES,
probe enables MQ with a default RX count of min(num_online_cpus(), 8)
(same cap as TX today); ethtool -L can raise RX up to 16. Packets are
received on per-queue NAPI. Older firmware without the bit is unchanged.
Queue selection remains firmware-defined (PHYP hash). Ethtool RSS hash
get/set for that algorithm is deferred to a follow-up series so this
one stays MQ datapath only.

User-visible bits: ethtool -l/-L (channels); standard per-queue
packets/bytes and RX drops via netdev_stat_ops (ethtool -S keeps only
driver-specific counters; ndo_get_stats64 is the aggregate, including
retired-queue history); and a read-only debugfs buffer_pools dump
(v3's multi-line sysfs dump moved to debugfs; the historical queue-0
poolN/ sysfs ABI is unchanged).

This version is based on net-next, which already includes the two
open() error-path fixes (af0524bf4ce1, 84bec0bf0352), with the
eight ibmveth fixes ("ibmveth: fix hangs, use-after-frees and
netpoll races", mailed v3; v2 was [1]) applied. Those fixes came
out of the review of v7; the MQ patches now extend that handling
per queue instead of carrying their own copy, so this series no
longer fixes single-queue bugs.

The eighth fix waits for the RX poll before freeing the RX queue.
Patch 2 keeps that wait on napi[0] until patch 5.

Background:

ibmveth today uses one logical LAN, one set of buffer pools, and one
NAPI context. PHYP MQ mode gives each RX queue its own handle (post via
H_ADD_LOGICAL_LAN_BUFFERS_QUEUE, subordinate register via
H_REG_LOGICAL_LAN_QUEUE); traffic can land on any active queue. The
driver needs per-queue pools, IRQs, and NAPI to match. Legacy firmware
keeps the original hcall path.

Series layout (15 patches):

  1-2   Hypercall wrappers; MQ adapter layout (MAX_RX_QUEUES stays 1)
  3-9   Queue-aware helpers (still SQ runtime): RX, per-queue pools,
        IRQ, TX, PHYP, buffer submit (open/close 3-8); poll harden (9)
  10    Enable MQ datapath at probe/open (subordinate register helpers
        land here with first use)
  11-13 Per-queue RX/TX stats; get_channels MQ counts; debugfs buffer_pools
  14    Incremental RX resize; live ethtool -L rx
  15    Down-path publish

- Helper patches (3-8) reshape ibmveth_open()/close() into
  queue-aware helpers. Patch 9 hardens the SQ poll path with the same
  queue-index helpers; it does not change open/close. MQ stays off
  through 3-9: num_rx_queues stays 1 and multi_queue is false until
  patch 10. The live single-queue path still changes where the review
  required it (IRQ teardown wait and remask, replenish lock, poll
  harden).
- Patch 10 is the switch: probe sets multi_queue from firmware, raises
  num_rx_queues, registers subordinates, and replenishes every active
  queue.
- Patch 11 moves counters per-queue and exports packets/bytes
  and RX drops through netdev_stat_ops. TX hw_drops is left
  unset. The thirteen existing -S keys stay; no
  hcall_* or pool%d_ keys.

Testing:

ppc64le PowerVM LPAR, MQ-capable firmware:
* ethtool -L cycling (16/1/8/11/1/3/16/8/1) with ping - no hangs
* ethtool -L under iperf3; link down/up during traffic
* ifdown/ifup under iperf3 RX+TX (MQ and ethtool -L rx 1)
* Legacy firmware (no MQ bit): open/close/stress on helper path
* Bisect-safe build and boot at every commit; W=1 clean at every
  commit
* KUnit (ibmveth-kunit-test) on qemu pseries (ppc64le)

Changes in v8:

Same 15 patches as v7, rebased onto the fixes above, plus the v7
netdev-bot review (Openwall 2026-09-29) and our own re-review.
Per-patch detail is below --- on each commit.

Dropped because the fixes now do it (the MQ patches extend the
fixes' code per queue instead):
* ndo_poll_controller and its per-queue rewrite, the netpoll kick and
  the closed-device guard (fix 1 removes ndo_poll_controller).
* The opened field and its close() / set_channels() gates (fix 2).
* The remove() unregister/cancel reorder and the reset
  NETREG_REGISTERED gate (fix 3 disables the work first).
* The correlator WARN_ON() conversion, the NULL-buffer advance and
  the copybreak skb leak (fix 4). Patches 8 and 9 make fix 4's
  helpers per-queue.
* The pool kobject release() and wait (fix 5). Patch 10 adds the
  per-queue pools to its helper.
* close()'s netif_tx_disable() (fix 7).
* The close()/open-fail wait for the RX poll tail (fix 8).
* The open() failure h_free_logical_lan() and the NULL-safe TX LTB
  free (the net pair).

Code changes:
* Patch 1: H_FREE_LOGICAL_LAN_QUEUE H_PARAMETER kdoc is
  already-free, same as H_FREE_LOGICAL_LAN. hcall_inst can count
  the new opcodes; a debugfs row appears only after a call.
* Patch 2: fix 8's synchronize_net() after napi_disable() stays
  on napi[0] until patch 5. Index 0 also covers the fixes-series
  helpers and their KUnit.
* Patch 4: a pool-alloc failure still calls h_free_logical_lan(),
  as the net fix does. v7 took the TX unwind and skipped it.
* Patch 5: cleanup_rx_interrupts() and the setup unwind wait for the
  poll tail after napi_disable(), then remask and free_irq; close()
  and open()'s unwinds need no wait of their own.
* Patch 6: netif_tx_disable() at the start of open(); start stays
  after TX LTB alloc. TX alloc stays after LAN registration.
* Patch 7: queue_registered[] is the slot-is-live flag. Handle 0 is
  a live PHYP cookie; do not treat it as unregistered.
* Patch 8: replenish_lock is spin_lock_bh(), so IRQs stay on
  during the fill. replenish_task_cycles++ under the lock; first
  BATCH_FALLBACK and HCALL_FAIL are logged after unlock. Harvest
  resets on a bad correlator.
* Patch 9: snapshot the correlator once in deliver_frame().
  Bad-correlator and oversize skips count rx_dropped and consume
  NAPI budget. rx_invalid_buffer stays the slots PHYP marks
  invalid. The stopping check only narrows the re-arm window;
  cleanup's synchronize_net() is the barrier.
* Patch 9: keep fix 1's rescheduled / budget-1 return on the
  napi_schedule restart path after rebase onto fixes v3.

* Patch 10: set_channels() returns -EOPNOTSUPP on an rx_count
  change until patch 14. apply_mq_fallback() sets real_num to 1.
  A hard-failed H_FREE skips unmap. Subordinate IRQs are named
  ibmveth-<unit>-rxN; queue 0 keeps netdev->name.
* Patch 11: snapshot no_buffer_drops and retired under
  replenish_lock; fold the last sample after successful H_FREE.
  Driver RX drops are per-queue and summed into rx_dropped.
  rx_missed_errors is the PHYP no-buffer pair. TX hw_drops left
  unset; dropped_packets stays ndo_get_stats64 tx_dropped only.
  Per-queue counters use WRITE_ONCE()/READ_ONCE() (single writer,
  lockless readers); rx%d_polls is not counted for netpoll's
  budget-0 call, so it keeps one writer. Per-queue -S keys that
  duplicate standard stats are gone: the no-buffer pair is qstats
  rx-hw-drop-overruns (and part of rx-hw-drops); tx%d_large_packets
  and tx%d_checksum_offload are dropped, and TX large sends stay in
  the existing tx_large_packets key. The no-buffer values no longer
  reset on close.
* Patch 12: cap max_rx at the live rx_count once mq_fallback.
  Do not advertise max_rx = 1. mq_fallback uses
  READ_ONCE()/WRITE_ONCE().
* Patch 14: scale-down, the scale-up unwind and the keep path wait
  for the poll tail with synchronize_net() after napi_disable(),
  then remask. Combined -L rolls RX back if TX misses. Skip unmask
  if set_real(keep) fails; still napi_enable so close/reset
  is not a second napi_disable hang. Close skips unmap on
  H_FREE fail-before-start and retries on the next open and
  remove (remove logs if it still fails and leaks the LAN
  buffers); until then a poolN sysfs write returns -EBUSY
  so the deferred free keeps the old pool geometry.
  H_FREE_LOGICAL_LAN_QUEUE H_PARAMETER is already-free, same as
  H_FREE_LOGICAL_LAN: clear the registered flag and unmap.
  napi_disable() on retiring queues keeps netpoll off them; poll
  records an RX index only below real_num. free_stranded keys off
  queue_registered[], not a zero handle, after successful H_FREE.
  Scale-down takes the final
  no_buffer sample after H_FREE. Moved here from patch 15, next to
  the code they fix: poll completion, interrupt and resume honor
  real_num; free_tx_resources() walks every LTB slot.
* Patch 15: down-path publish returns -EBUSY if a queue in the new
  range is still registered. resize_rx_channels kdoc matches the
  down-path publish. mq_fallback max_rx cap lives in patch 12.
* Commit message / kdoc / comment updates on every patch,
  mostly to drop what the fixes now own.
* Kept: enable_irq on schedule_prep failure; mask PHYP before
  napi_disable; get_channels reports the live rx_count (no clamp);
  ethtool -L TX shrink stops the queues and relies on
  netif_set_real_num_tx_queues()'s synchronize_net() before the LTBs
  are freed.

Known leftovers (not this series):

* ip_fast_csum() on IHL=0. Preexisting RX. Not this series.
* A PHYP mask hcall that failed is still never acknowledged.
* The v7 single-queue replenish leftover (irqsave over the whole
  fill, netpoll vs close) is gone: fix 1 takes netpoll out of
  replenish and patch 8 uses spin_lock_bh().

Changes in v7:

Same 15 patches as v6. We followed up the v6 netdev-bot review
with replies; this v7 is the series after that, plus a few items
from our own re-review.

* Patch 3: update_rx_no_buffer() returns if buffer_list_addr[0] is
  NULL (the per-queue form stays in patch 10).
* Patch 6: synchronize_net() on the late TX-alloc unwind before RX is
  freed.
* Patch 8: replenish failure log names the wrapper from the filled
  count; advance ring on NULL buffer so poll does not spin.
* Patch 9: oversize bound is min(skb_tailroom, pool->buff_size).
* Patch 10: unregister_netdev before cancel_work_sync and gate reset
  on NETREG_REGISTERED (moved from patch 11); wait for pool kobject
  release before free_netdev(). Drop the probe CMO refresh and the two
  CMO follow-ups: CMO (Power9 and earlier) and MQ firmware (Power11+)
  do not coexist.
* Patch 11: replenish_lock on the close harvest (remove-path
  unregister/cancel reorder moved to patch 10 with the reset producer).
* Patch 12: set_channels() returns -EOPNOTSUPP on rx_count changes until
  patch 14 implements live resize.
* Patch 14: key buffer-list unmap on allocation presence because
  DMA address zero is valid; reject an RX count change while down
  with -EOPNOTSUPP. Failed H_FREE skips unmap and restores the
  surviving count; widen real_num before scale-up unmask. Scale-up
  register -EOPNOTSUPP latches mq_fallback. The scale-up /
  scale-down helper split is code motion only.
* Patch 15: publish the down-path RX count, which lifts patch 14's
  temporary rejection. get_channels max_tx is at least the live
  tx_count, and set_channels uses the same ceiling, so CPU offline
  cannot block an RX-only ethtool -L.
* Commit message / kdoc / comment / debug-log updates on 1, 3, 4, 5,
  7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 14 and 15 (patch 1 also names the new hcalls in the perf
  powerpc-hcalls script and documents H_BUSY on the register-queue
  wrapper; patch 10 prints the register-queue failure with %ld).
* Kept: enable_irq on schedule_prep failure; mask PHYP before
  napi_disable; get_channels reports the live rx_count (no clamp).
* Reopen unwind in patches 3 and 6 is pre-existing. No Fixes: tag
  here. The SQ open-fail path is already on the list as
  [PATCH net 0/2] (Message-ID:
  <cover.1790357373.git.mmc@linux.ibm.com>). This series does
  not depend on it. If both land, keep the helper versions in
  patches 3/4/6; the net pair is the current single-queue path
  only.

Changes in v6:

Same 15 patches as v5. Jakub v5 review folded in; per-patch detail is
below --- on each commit.

* Both new registration wrappers use plpar_hcall(), not plpar_hcall9().
* Poll: IPv4 check through skb->data; budget 0 does not complete NAPI.
* Scale-down: publish the surviving count, then synchronize_net(),
  then destroy. num_rx_queues uses smp_store_release / smp_load_acquire.
* packets/bytes/drops through netdev_stat_ops, not private -S strings.
  Thirteen existing -S keys kept. No hcall_* or pool%d_ keys.
  replenish_* are per-queue u64; no atomics. get_base_stats() is the
  retired-queue remainder.
* Reset worker gated on NETREG_REGISTERED (cannot reopen after
  unregister).
* get_channels() keeps the live rx_count; mq_fallback caps max_rx so
  a TX-only ethtool -L is not a silent RX shrink.

